# NETracer: A Topology-Aware Iterative Tracing Approach for Tubular Structure Extraction

Chao Liu1, Yangbo Jiang2, Nenggan Zheng1, 3, 4 1Zhejiang University, China

2Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics, China 3Collaborative Innovation Center for Artificial Intelligence by MOE and Zhejiang Provincial Government, China 4Bengbu University, China

supermeliu@zju.edu.cn, jiangyangbo@zufe.edu.cn, zng@cs.zju.edu.cn

## Abstract

Extracting tubular structures from images is a widespread and challenging task in computer vision. To explore these continuous structures, iterative tracing methods offer a promising direction. However, in scenes with dense and blurred branches, existing tracing methods tend to jump to adjacent branches during the tracing process, leading to a significant topological mistake. The reason for this shortcoming is that the tracing model only focuses on the estimation of discrete nodes and ignores their connection attribution. To solve this problem, we introduce NETracer, a topology-aware iterative tracing method to improve the continuity and topological accuracy. In our approach, a node-edge estimation network with local connectivity loss is trained to produce the future nodes and their connective edges. Then, a geodesic distance-based search strategy is employed with the help of predicted edge cues to trace the future branches more accurately. Additionally, to comprehensively assess the effect of the tracing model, a new tracing metric is proposed to evaluate the local accuracy, continuity, and topological correctness of the traced branches. We demonstrate that our method outperforms existing segmentation and tracing methods on five 2D road, vessel, and 3D neuron datasets. The code of NETracer is available at https://github.com/CSDLLab/NETracer.

## 1. Introduction

Tubular structure extraction is a crucial and challenging task in computer vision, such as the reconstruction of neuronal dendrites or axons [15, 24], the segmentation of retinal vessels [28], and the delineation of road networks from aerial imagery [5, 33]. These tubular structure can be represented as a series of nodes and edges connecting them, and our objective is to precisely extract these continuous objects to align with the image evidence.

