(d) Ours Proposed Method  
Figure 1. Motivation. (a) 3D neuron image and ground truth. (b) Segmentation results misinterpret ambiguous branches as a single entity. (c) Existing tracing approaches overlook the connectivity between discrete nodes, resulting in topological errors. (d) Integrating edge information can improve the topological accuracy.

Most existing methods tackle this problem as a twostage segmentation task: first, employing a segmentation network [13, 25, 27, 44] to learn the centerline features, followed by the use of a post-processing algorithm [11, 12, 19, 32] to extract the topological connectivity patterns. However, the major drawback of these approaches is that they place significant reliance on the segmentation results from the first stage. When the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of image evidence is low, segmentation results may exhibit merging or blurring in localized regions, as illustrated in Fig. 1 (b). This issue can mislead post-processing algorithms into producing shortcut topological errors.

In contrast, iterative tracing approaches provide a more streamlined method to explicitly model the continuity of the branches. They start from a set of seed nodes and progressively explore unknown branches. The key to these methods lies in estimating the position of next node to drive the iterative process. This encompasses estimating the branch direction [1, 4, 14] and deriving the future node position from a probability map [10, 30]. However, the shortcoming of these approaches is that they treat the extraction of continuous curves as a discrete node prediction task, lacking the ability to learn the connectivity features between adjacent nodes. When encountering scenarios where two branches are closely spaced or entangled, multiple potential future nodes may become available for selection, as shown in Fig. 1 (c). Thus, a natural question will be raised: How to choose the most suitable future node?

To address this challenge, we introduce NETracer, a novel topology-aware iterative tracing model. Our proposed method aims to model the tubular structure as continuous sub-segments rather than discrete nodes, thereby integrating the local connectivity patterns of these nodes into the tracing process. Firstly, we theoretically analyze the limitations of existing tracing methods, and propose a new optimization objective that emphasizes branch continuity. Following this criterion, a node-edge estimation network (NENet) is proposed which includes three subtask learning blocks: centerline, future node, and future edge. The additional future edge information can provide crucial connectivity cues for guiding the selection of future nodes. Notably, a local connectivity loss is added in the future edge estimation block to ensure the reliability of local edge prediction. In the tracing process, a geodesic distance-based search strategy (GDS) is developed to select the most suitable future node from several potential node sets with the predicted edge cues. Finally, since there are few evaluation metrics for tracing models, we propose an iterative tracing metric that encompasses three sub-metrics to evaluate the continuity and topological accuracy of the tracing results. Our contributions are summarized as follows:

• We introduce NETracer, a novel topology-aware iterative tracing model. It seamlessly integrates connectivity features into the tracing process for the first time.

• NETracer employs a NENet with a local connectivity loss to produce future nodes and edges. Then, a GDS strategy is applied to pinpoint the most suitable future node based on the predicted edge cues.

• A brand-new iterative tracing metric is introduced to assess the local position accuracy, continuity, and topological accuracy of the tracing model.

• The extensive experiments on five 2D and 3D datasets show that our NETracer model surpasses other state-ofthe-art (SOTA) segmentation and tracing methods.

## 2. Related work

## 2.1. Two-stage Segmentation Approaches

As mentioned in the previous section, these two-stage methods divide the feature learning of tubular structure into two distinct phases. Firstly, a segmentation network is trained to learn the centerline features. Then, a post-processing algorithm is applied to extract their topological connectivity patterns. In recent years, various centerline segmentation networks have demonstrated significant progress in addressing complex tubular structure extraction tasks and can be categorized into two primary groups. One category focuses on enhancing the network backbone, with notable contributions from the incorporation of attention mechanisms [20], deformable convolutions [6, 7, 25], and graph convolutions [41, 43], among others. Other approaches focus on refining downstream tasks or imposing specialized loss functions, including the topological constraint loss [27, 37], regional connectivity [21, 31], and tubular flux features learning block [34].

However, segmentation results can only provide a binary mask of the tubular structure, which is insufficient for constructing the final representation. To address this, various post-processing techniques have been developed. APP and APP2 [23, 38] apply a fast marching method using geodesic distance to reconstruct grayscale neuron images. Path-CNN [11] employs a progressive minimal path method on the segmentation centerline, providing geometric cues to improve performance. These methods directly extract curves from a global predicted centerline map without imposing any topological constraints, resulting in low reliability of the local structures. As an improved method, [19] extracts all significant nodes and applies the A\* algorithm to connect them into an overcomplete graph. Subsequently, a path classifier is trained to determine whether the given path exists. However, this method still faces the same challenge: its path classifier must be an extremely robust binary classification model. Otherwise, the probability of generating shortcut branches rises significantly

## 2.2. Iterative Tracing Approaches

Iterative tracing approaches aim to estimate the position of future nodes to finish the tracing process. To capture the continuous features of branches, some methods rely on parametric geometric models designed based on prior knowledge. neuTu [42] utilizes a cylinder template to fit the branch in the image. [17, 36] employs a ray burst sampling method to recursively explore along the neurite branches. [26] proposes the voxel-scooping spherical model to iteratively carve out the neuron branch. However, these methods are only effective in certain applications and rely on manually crafted features, making it difficult to tune parameters when dealing with different datasets.

With the advent of deep learning networks, RoadTracer [1] and SPE [4] construct the CNN-based models to learn the direction and radius of branches to estimate the next iteration step. However, the estimation of these high-level semantic features of branches consistently leads to positional errors contrasted with the ground truth, and this deviation tends to increase throughout the iterative tracing process. To enhance the accuracy of the tracing process, DeepBranch-Tracer [14] takes global centerline features into consideration to adjust the position of predicted future nodes at each step, effectively increasing the continuity of the branches. As another simplified solution, Vec-Road [30] proposes that future nodes can be obtained directly from the peak of the predicted position distribution probability maps. Nonetheless, all of these tracing approaches do not consider the connectivity feature of two neighboring nodes, and the predicted nodes may jump to other adjacent branches, leading to significant topological errors.

## 3. Method

In this section, we formulate the tubular structure tracing problem as an optimization objective, encompassing the posterior probability of historical paths and the continuity of branches. Based on this principle, we propose the NENet to learn the local branch attributions, which integrate the centerline, future node, and future edge blocks with a topologyaware constraint loss. In the tracing process, a geodesic distance-based search strategy is proposed and can search along the edge probability map to candidate the most suitable future node from the several potential future nodes.

## 3.1. Problem Formulation

A tubular structure is a continuous curve traversing 3D space, formally denoted as $\{ \mathbf { c } ( t ) = ( x ( t ) , y ( t ) , z ( t ) ) ^ { \bar { T } } , t \in$ $( 0 , 1 ) \} \in C$ , where $C \subset \mathbb { R } ^ { 3 }$ . The extraction of tubular structure is trying to seek the optimal curve c given the image evidence I, and can be represented as $P ( \mathbf { c } | I )$ . For the tracing models, they discretized this curve into M discrete nodes $\mathbf { c } ( t _ { i } )$ and the tracing task can be formulated within a probabilistic framework:

$$
P ( \mathbf { c } | I ) \propto P ( \mathbf { c } ( t _ { 1 } ) | I ) \prod _ { i = 2 } ^ { M } P \left( \mathbf { c } ( t _ { i } ) | W [ \mathbf { c } ( t _ { 1 } ) , \cdots , \mathbf { c } ( t _ { i - 1 } ) ] , I \right) ,\tag{1}
$$

where I is the input image. The first term $P ( \mathbf { c } ( t _ { 1 } ) | I )$ represents the initial seed node for the tracing process, and the second term can be represented as estimating the future nodes by maximizing a posterior probability given the image evidence I and the historical path W. Thus, the optimization objective for iterative tracing approaches can be

written as:

$$
c ^ { * } = { \underset { \mathbf { c } ( t _ { i } ) \in C } { \operatorname { a r g m a x } } } P ( \mathbf { c } ( t _ { i } ) | W [ \mathbf { c } ( t _ { 1 } ) , \cdots , \mathbf { c } ( t _ { i - 1 } ) ] , I ) .\tag{2}
$$

However, as an approximate optimization objective, the continuity and connectivity between two neighboring nodes $\mathbf { c } ( t _ { i } )$ and $\mathbf { c } ( t _ { i - 1 } )$ is ignored, leading to critical topological errors in the tracing scenario of two adjacent branches, as shown in Fig. 1(c). To tackle this problem, a better tracing model should estimate not just the future nodes but also the edges that interconnect them, aiming to enhance the topological integrity. Building upon this concept, a new optimization objective can be formalized as follows:

$$
c ^ { * } = \underset { { \bf c } ( t _ { i } ) \in C } { \arg \operatorname* { m a x } } \int _ { t _ { i - 1 } } ^ { t _ { i } } P ( { \bf c } ( t ) | W [ { \bf c } ( t _ { 1 } ) , \cdot \cdot \cdot , { \bf c } ( t _ { i - 1 } ) ] , I ) \mathrm { d } t ,\tag{3}
$$

where the integral from $t _ { i - 1 }$ to $t _ { i }$ on the curve can capture the probability of the continuous edge from the current node $\mathbf { c } ( t _ { i - 1 } )$ to the future node $\mathbf { c } ( t _ { i } )$ . Based on this criterion, the model can learn to predict future nodes that not only individually align well with the image evidence, but also form a sequence that adheres to the expected topology.

## 3.2. NENet Architecture

Following the above optimization objective, the design of a deep network for iterative tracing can be decoupled into three subtasks:

• Exploring Future Nodes: The primary task of an iterative tracing model is to explore future nodes along the branch.
